# ============================================================================
# EXERCISE 5: VIRTUAL INSTANCES, SERVER CONSOLIDATION, FAULT TOLERANCE
# All steps below are performed in VMware Workstation and Ubuntu terminal.
# PART 1 - VIRTUAL INSTANCES
# 1. Open VMware Workstation Pro 17 and start the existing Ubuntu 24.04.1 LTS VM.
# 2. Right-click the Ubuntu VM > Manage > Clone > Create a full clone.
#    Create two or three additional VMs if resources permit.
# 3. Power on each cloned VM and open Terminal in each one.
# 4. On EACH web-server VM, run these commands:
#    sudo apt update
#    sudo apt install apache2 -y
#    sudo systemctl enable --now apache2
#    sudo systemctl status apache2
# 5. To customize the web page on each VM:
#    sudo nano /var/www/html/index.html
#    Edit the HTML so each VM displays a distinct name, then save with Ctrl+O,
#    press Enter, and exit with Ctrl+X.
# 6. Find the VM's IP address with: hostname -I
#    (The manual lists ifconfig; if unavailable, install net-tools or use hostname -I.)
# 7. Test each Apache server from a browser: http://<VM_IP>
#
# PART 1 - TASK 2: HAPROXY LOAD BALANCER
# 1. Use a separate Ubuntu VM as the load balancer (or use one VM if necessary).
# 2. Install HAProxy:
#    sudo apt update
#    sudo apt install haproxy -y
# 3. Back up and edit the configuration:
#    sudo cp /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg.bak
#    sudo nano /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg
# 4. Add this frontend/backend configuration to the file (replace IP placeholders):
#    frontend http_front
#       bind *:80
#       default_backend http_back
#
#    backend http_back
#       balance roundrobin
#       server vm1 <VM1_IP>:80 check
#       server vm2 <VM2_IP>:80 check
#       server vm3 <VM3_IP>:80 check
# 5. Validate and restart HAProxy:
#    sudo haproxy -c -f /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg
#    sudo systemctl restart haproxy
#    sudo systemctl enable haproxy
#    sudo systemctl status haproxy
# 6. Test in a browser at http://<LOAD_BALANCER_IP>.
#    The manual's example URL is http://192.168.119.128; use your actual IP.
#
# PART 2 - SERVER CONSOLIDATION
# 1. In VMware Workstation, review each VM's CPU, memory, and storage allocation.
# 2. Identify low-usage/redundant VMs and choose a host with enough resources.
# 3. Open VM > Edit virtual machine settings and adjust CPU and memory sensibly.
# 4. Copy files/services to the consolidated VM and start/test those services there.
# 5. After verifying migrated services, power off unused original VMs; remove them
#    only if you are sure they are no longer needed.
# 6. Check resource use in Ubuntu with: top
#    Optional: install and use htop with: sudo apt install htop -y && htop
#
# PART 3 - FAULT TOLERANCE
# 1. Clone a critical VM: VM > Manage > Clone > Full Clone.
# 2. Synchronize data with rsync (replace paths, username, and backup IP):
#    rsync -av /source_directory/ user@<BACKUP_VM_IP>:/destination_directory
# 3. In VMware Workstation, select a critical VM > VM > Snapshot > Take Snapshot.
#    Example name: Pre-Consolidation Snapshot. Take snapshots before major changes.
# RESULT: VM instances, load balancing, consolidation, and recovery preparation.
